---
/**
 * A single project, presented as an entry in an engineering notebook rather
 * than as a SaaS card.
 *
 * ## The whole card is the link
 *
 * The title carries the one real `<a>`; a `::after` pseudo-element stretches
 * that anchor over the entire card. So the card has exactly **one** tab stop
 * for "open the case study", and its accessible name is the project title —
 * not "View project", repeated four times down a listing.
 *
 * The visible `VIEW PROJECT →` line is `aria-hidden` on purpose. It is the
 * sighted affordance for a link that already exists; promoting it to a second
 * anchor would duplicate the destination in the tab order and in the a11y tree.
 *
 * `github` / `demo` are genuinely different destinations, so they stay real
 * anchors. They are lifted above the stretched overlay with `z-index`, and
 * because they sit after the title in DOM order the tab sequence inside one
 * card is always:
 *
 *   1. card (title link → case study)
 *   2. GitHub   (only when `links.github` exists)
 *   3. Live demo (only when `links.demo` exists)
 *
 * ## Nothing is hover-only
 *
 * Every piece of information — summary, domains, stack, role, year, links — is
 * in the resting state. Hover and focus change *emphasis*, never content, and
 * `:focus-within` is listed beside `:hover` on every rule in this file, so a
 * keyboard user sees exactly what a pointer user sees — with one difference, by
 * construction: a pointer also decides where the light falls. Nothing is
 * conditional on it. On touch (`hover: none`) the card simply starts in the
 * stronger state (brief §33, MOTION_SYSTEM §7).
 *
 * ## Motion — one object, one physical event
 *
 * **A sheet of paper lifted off the page under a raking light whose source is
 * the cursor.** That single sentence is the whole interaction, and every
 * declaration below is a consequence of it rather than an effect in its own
 * right: the sheet rises 3px, it grows a hairline edge and casts an ink
 * shadow, and the light falling across it is brightest where the pointer is.
 * MOTION_SYSTEM §9 allows "subtle depth **or** spotlight, not both stacked" —
 * this is not two stacked effects but one lit, raised surface, which is why it
 * is a single pseudo-element with a single opacity transition.
 *
 * Deliberately absent, and each for a reason rather than for restraint alone:
 * tilt (fights the "flat sheet on a desk" reading the whole card is built on,
 * and §9 caps it at 2–3° where it would read as noise anyway), media zoom
 * (every cover is a figure whose axis labels are the evidence — see
 * `.card__media`, which contains rather than crops precisely so nothing is
 * lost), glare sweep, and metadata reveal.
 *
 * ### What the previous version got wrong
 *
 * The same structure was already here and was, measured on a real build,
 * invisible: the "spotlight" was a pool of `--color-paper-raised` on the
 * `--color-paper` ground, two values a fraction of a step apart, and there was
 * no shadow and no edge at all. The visible half of a hover was a 2px text
 * shift. Three things fix it, none of which is a new colour:
 *
 *   1. the pool now runs from `--color-paper-raised` at the light down to a
 *      7% `--color-ink` mix away from it, so the sheet is *shaded* rather than
 *      uniformly tinted, and the gradient has somewhere to travel;
 *   2. a real drop shadow, mixed from `--color-ink`, because "lifted" without
 *      a shadow is just a 3px jump;
 *   3. a hairline edge, so the sheet has a boundary the eye can find.
 *
 * ### The pointer, and the users who do not have one
 *
 * `--card-x` / `--card-y` position the light. They default to `18% 0%` in this
 * stylesheet — upper left, the conventional light direction — and
 * `ProjectGrid.astro` overwrites them per card, through CSSOM, only for a
 * visitor with a fine pointer and motion enabled.
 *
 * So the resting composition is already a *complete* lit sheet. Keyboard,
 * touch, reduced motion and no-JavaScript all get the identical shadow, edge,
 * gradient and colour response; the only thing they do not get is a light that
 * moves. That is the inverse of the usual arrangement, where the pointer user
 * gets the design and everyone else gets the leftovers, and it is what lets
 * `:focus-within` stay byte-identical to `:hover` while the pointer effect
 * still exists.
 *
 * The custom properties are written with `element.style.setProperty()`, which
 * is CSSOM and permitted, and consumed by the completely static rule below.
 * A `style=""` **attribute** would be dropped by the CSP — see the long note
 * above `.card__title-text`.
 *
 * ## Strings and colour
 *
 * Every user-facing string, including `aria-label` and `alt`, comes from `t`
 * (audit 2.8). No colour literal appears here; domain accents are read as
 * `var(--color-domain-*)` through the `accentVar` token name that
 * `src/config/domains.ts` owns.
 */

import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { domainOrdinal, getDomain } from '@/config/domains';
import { localizePath, type Locale, type UIStrings } from '@/i18n';
import type { Project } from '@/lib/projects';
import { isWorkInProgress } from '@/lib/projects/visibility';

interface Props {
  /** The joined project: shared metadata plus the localized title and summary. */
  project: Project;
  /** The dictionary. Components never look translations up themselves. */
  t: UIStrings;
  /** Needed as well as `base` — audit 1.3 was a component that only had `base`. */
  locale: Locale;
  /** Deployment base path. Every href goes through `localizePath`. */
  base: string;
  /**
   * Heading level for the card title. The surrounding section owns the level
   * above it; passing this is how the projects index stops skipping h1 → h3
   * (audit 2.2).
   */
  headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4 | undefined;
  /**
   * Position in the listing, rendered as a two-digit figure number in the
   * margin. Locale-independent and `aria-hidden`, so it is annotation rather
   * than an untranslated string.
   */
  index?: number | undefined;
  /**
   * This card holds the first cover in its listing, so that cover is the page's
   * likely Largest Contentful Paint and must not be lazy-loaded.
   *
   * There used to be an `emphasis` prop beside this one, promoting a listing's
   * opener to a larger title and the full measure, and the two were kept apart
   * deliberately: emphasis was an editorial decision a listing could decline to
   * make, while loading priority is a fact about which card holds the first
   * image. `ProjectGrid` no longer promotes anything, so only the fact remains.
   */
  priority?: boolean | undefined;
}

const { project, t, locale, base, headingLevel = 3, index, priority = false } = Astro.props;
const { meta, title, summary } = project;

const HEADINGS = { 2: 'h2', 3: 'h3', 4: 'h4' } as const;
const Heading = HEADINGS[headingLevel];

const href = localizePath(`/projects/${meta.slug}`, locale, base);

/**
 * Cover images are stored as a path string (`"media/cover.webp"`), not as an
 * `image()` schema field, because `src/content/schema.ts` deliberately imports
 * no Astro virtual module. Resolving them through `import.meta.glob` gives back
 * real `ImageMetadata` — which is what `<Image />` needs in order to emit
 * `width`/`height` and a `srcset`.
 *
 * The audit's MEDIUM performance finding was a raw `<img>` with no dimensions.
 * Raster formats only — `astro:assets` does not transform SVG, and a diagram
 * cover would want different framing anyway.
 */
const covers = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>(
  '/src/content/projects/*/media/*.{webp,avif,png,jpg,jpeg}',
  { eager: true },
);
const cover = meta.cover ? covers[`/src/content/projects/${meta.slug}/${meta.cover}`]?.default : undefined;

/**
 * The candidate widths, filtered against what the source actually has.
 *
 * `<Image />` will happily generate a 1080px variant of a 431px source: sharp
 * upscales, so the page pays for bytes that carry no detail and renders a soft
 * image on the display that asked for them. Every real cover here is a plot
 * exported at whatever size its notebook chose, so the set is intersected with
 * the intrinsic width rather than assumed. A source narrower than the smallest
 * candidate still gets a one-entry `srcset` at its own width — correct, and
 * honest about the resolution that exists.
 */
const RESPONSIVE_WIDTHS = [480, 768, 1080, 1344];
const fitted = cover ? RESPONSIVE_WIDTHS.filter((width) => width <= cover.width) : [];
const coverWidths = fitted.length > 0 ? fitted : cover ? [cover.width] : [];

/**
 * `sizes`, which depends on whether this card is the lead.
 *
 * A lead card spans the whole grid (`grid__item--lead` is `grid-column: 1/-1`);
 * every other card is one of two columns. Those are very different slots — 1344
 * against 640 at a 1440px viewport — and one `sizes` string cannot describe
 * both. It used to, because it did not have to: no project shipped a cover, and
 * then the first one to do so was not the lead.
 *
 * It is the lead now, and a `sizes` that understates its slot is not a rounding
 * error. Measured on a real build before this line existed: the opening plot
 * laid out at 1342px and loaded the 768w variant, a 1.75× upscale of the
 * largest, most-scrutinised image on the site — soft in exactly the place the
 * whole media pass exists to be sharp. `fetchpriority="high"` had faithfully
 * rushed the wrong file.
 *
 * The measured widths, from `.card__media` at each breakpoint: 95vw in one
 * column, ~45vw once the grid is two, settling at 40rem once the container
 * caps. One expression rather than two, because every card is now the same
 * width — the second branch existed only for the full-measure lead card.
 * `sizes` is allowed to overstate — that costs bytes — but understating costs
 * resolution, so each stop is rounded up rather than down.
 */
const coverSizes = '(min-width: 84rem) 40rem, (min-width: 48rem) 45vw, 95vw';

/**
 * Which reserved box this cover gets.
 *
 * The covers are figures, not photographs, and their aspect ratios are set by
 * matplotlib rather than by a designer: a 14-day forecast plot is 2.5:1, a 6×6
 * confusion matrix is 1.1:1. One fixed box plus `object-fit: cover` would crop
 * the axis labels off the first and the class labels off the second, and
 * `fill` would squash both. Neither is acceptable for evidence.
 *
 * So the box is chosen from a *closed* set of two ratios and the image is
 * always `contain`ed inside it: nothing is ever cropped, nothing is ever
 * distorted, the ratio is known at build time so CLS stays at zero, and a
 * listing can only ever contain two media heights rather than one per project.
 * The leftover is paper, which is what the rest of the card is made of.
 *
 * A data attribute rather than a computed `aspect-ratio`, because the CSP
 * drops inline `style` attributes — see the long note above `.card__title-text`.
 */
const coverShape = cover && cover.width / cover.height >= 2 ? 'wide' : 'standard';

/**
 * Alt text for a project image.
 *
 * `project.json` carries no `alt` field and must not grow one: alt is
 * user-facing copy, and every user-facing string on this site lives in the
 * dictionaries (`src/i18n/types.ts`; audit 2.8 was 14 of them hardcoded in
 * components). The key is `<slug>/<file stem>`, so a project with two figures
 * gets two descriptions rather than one title reused for images that show
 * different things.
 *
 * `mediaAlt` is an open `Record<string, string>` — it has to be, since the keys
 * are filenames — so under `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` a lookup is
 * `string | undefined` no matter how complete the dictionaries are. That
 * `undefined` must go somewhere, and the choice is not cosmetic:
 *
 *   · `?? ''` renders `alt=""`, which is the ARIA convention for a *decorative*
 *     image. Announcing a chart that carries the project's evidence as
 *     decoration is the one outcome worse than describing it vaguely, and it
 *     fails silently, to the readers least able to notice.
 *   · `!` asserts the case away without handling it, which is how the four
 *     latent defects in this component's media branch survived to begin with.
 *   · The localized title is vague but never wrong, and it is the alt
 *     convention `docs/PROJECT_CONTENT_CONTRACT.md` §10 already documents.
 *
 * So the fallback stays, and `tests/i18n.test.ts` is what makes sure it never
 * actually fires: that suite sweeps every project's `media/` directory and
 * fails unless each committed figure has a non-empty entry in both locales, no
 * orphans. The test guarantees the quality; the fallback guarantees the floor.
 * They are not redundant — one runs over the repo at build time, the other
 * covers the type hole that remains at runtime.
 */
const coverKey = meta.cover ? `${meta.slug}/${meta.cover.replace(/^media\//, '').replace(/\.\w+$/, '')}` : undefined;
const coverAlt = (coverKey === undefined ? undefined : t.projects.mediaAlt[coverKey]) ?? title;

const domains = meta.domains.map((id) => {
  const domain = getDomain(id);
  return { id, ordinal: domainOrdinal(domain), accentVar: domain.accentVar, name: t.worlds.items[id].name };
});

/** Role and year are optional content. Absent means absent — never a placeholder. */
const credits: string[] = [];
if (meta.role) credits.push(meta.role);
if (meta.year !== undefined) credits.push(String(meta.year));

const links = meta.links;
const external = [
  links?.github ? { href: links.github, text: t.projects.github, label: t.a11y.projectGithubLabel(title) } : null,
  links?.demo ? { href: links.demo, text: t.projects.demo, label: t.a11y.projectDemoLabel(title) } : null,
].filter((link): link is NonNullable<typeof link> => link !== null);

const figure = index === undefined ? undefined : String(index).padStart(2, '0');

/**
 * View-transition idents, carried as plain data attributes.
 *
 * `SLUG_PATTERN` forbids anything outside `[a-z0-9-]` and the literal prefix
 * keeps the result a valid `<custom-ident>` even for a slug starting with a
 * digit, so these are safe to read straight into `view-transition-name`.
 *
 * They are attributes rather than an inline `style` because the site ships a
 * hash-based CSP — see the long note above `.card__title-text` for why that
 * distinction is load-bearing rather than stylistic.
 */
const titleTransition = `project-title-${meta.slug}`;
const mediaTransition = `project-media-${meta.slug}`;
---

{
  /* `data-card-light` is the hook `ProjectGrid.astro`'s pointer script looks
     for. A data attribute rather than the `card` class so the enhancement is
     addressed by role, not by styling: a listing that ever renders a card
     variant which should not track the pointer simply omits it. */
}
<article class:list={['card', !cover && 'card--typographic']} data-project={meta.slug} data-card-light>
  {
    /* The card's masthead rule: one tinted segment per technical domain, then a
      hairline running out to the measure. A datasheet channel legend. It is
      redundant with the domain labels below, hence aria-hidden. */
  }
  <div class="card__channels" aria-hidden="true">
    {domains.map((domain) => <span class="card__channel" data-domain={domain.id} />)}
    <span class="card__channel-rule"></span>
    {figure && <span class="card__figure">{figure}</span>}
  </div>

  {
    cover && (
      <div class="card__media" data-shape={coverShape} data-vt={mediaTransition}>
        {/* Loading priority is per-card, not hardcoded.
             `loading="lazy"` on every cover was free while no project shipped
             one; the moment real media lands, the opening card's cover becomes
             the LCP element of both the projects index and the homepage's
             projects band, and a lazy-loaded LCP image is discovered only
             after layout instead of by the preload scanner.

             `fetchpriority="high"` is the other half. `loading="eager"` alone
             only removes the deferral; it does not lift the image above the
             low-priority default the browser gives images during initial
             layout.

             Everything below the opener stays lazy, and the reserved
             `aspect-ratio` box under `.card__media img` means neither branch
             can shift the page — CLS stays at zero either way. */}
        <Image
          src={cover}
          alt={coverAlt}
          widths={coverWidths}
          sizes={coverSizes}
          loading={priority ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
          fetchpriority={priority ? 'high' : 'auto'}
          decoding="async"
        />
      </div>
    )
  }

  <Heading class="card__title">
    <a class="card__link" href={href}><span class="card__title-text" data-vt={titleTransition}>{title}</span></a>
  </Heading>

  {
    /* The status badge, and the reason it is here rather than only on the
       detail page. `isVisible()` publishes a `wip` project into every listing
       alongside finished ones, and until now the only place saying so was the
       detail page — so a card in the grid presented unfinished work as though
       it were done, and a reader learned otherwise only after clicking. The
       predicate and the string both already existed; only the card was silent.

       Same predicate and same wording as the detail page, so the two cannot
       drift into disagreeing about what "in progress" means. */
    isWorkInProgress(project) && (
      <p class="card__status">
        <span class="card__status-dot" aria-hidden="true" />
        {t.projects.workInProgress}
      </p>
    )
  }

  <p class="card__summary">{summary}</p>

  {credits.length > 0 && <p class="card__credits">{credits.join(' · ')}</p>}

  <ul class="card__tags card__tags--domains" aria-label={t.projects.domains}>
    {
      domains.map((domain) => (
        <li class="card__domain" data-domain={domain.id}>
          <span class="card__domain-mark" aria-hidden="true" />
          {domain.name}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <ul class="card__tags card__tags--stack" aria-label={t.projects.stack}>
    {meta.stack.map((entry) => <li class="card__stack-item">{entry}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <div class="card__foot">
    <p class="card__cta" aria-hidden="true">
      {t.projects.viewProject}<span class="card__arrow">&#8594;</span>
    </p>

    {
      external.length > 0 && (
        <ul class="card__links" aria-label={t.projects.links}>
          {external.map((link) => (
            <li>
              <a class="card__external" href={link.href} aria-label={link.label}>
                {link.text}
                <span class="card__external-mark" aria-hidden="true">
                  &#8599;
                </span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )
    }
  </div>
</article>

<style>
  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Domain accents

     Resolved from `data-domain` here rather than from an inline `style` on each
     element. That is a CSP requirement, not a preference: `astro.config.mjs`
     enables `security.csp`, Astro emits `style-src` with hashes and **no**
     `style-src-attr` and no `'unsafe-hashes'`, and a hash never authorises a
     style *attribute* — only a `<style>` element. Every inline `style=""` on
     the page is therefore dropped in production while working perfectly in
     `astro dev`. Painting the accents that way shipped a card whose masthead
     channels and domain markers were invisible on the live site.

     Same pattern as the Technical Worlds island. `--domain-accent` is consumed
     only as a `background-color` mix; see the NON-TEXT ONLY notes below.

     These six lines mirror `Domain['accentVar']`, typed
     `--color-domain-${DomainId}`. CSS cannot derive the name, so a sixth domain
     needs a sixth line — `tests/project-card.test.ts` fails if one is missing.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .card [data-domain='product'] {
    --domain-accent: var(--color-domain-product);
  }
  .card [data-domain='ai'] {
    --domain-accent: var(--color-domain-ai);
  }
  .card [data-domain='quantum'] {
    --domain-accent: var(--color-domain-quantum);
  }
  .card [data-domain='fpga'] {
    --domain-accent: var(--color-domain-fpga);
  }
  .card [data-domain='electronics'] {
    --domain-accent: var(--color-domain-electronics);
  }
  .card [data-domain='audio'] {
    --domain-accent: var(--color-domain-audio);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     The card box
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .card {
    /* THE LIGHT SOURCE. Overwritten per card, through CSSOM, by the pointer
       script in `ProjectGrid.astro`; this pair is what every other input
       method sees, so it has to be a good composition on its own rather than a
       neutral placeholder. Upper left is the conventional direction for light
       falling on a raised surface.

       Kept in sync with `RESTING_LIGHT` in `src/lib/motion/card-light.ts`,
       which is what the script resets to when the pointer leaves —
       `tests/project-card.test.ts` fails if the two drift. */
    --card-x: 18%;
    --card-y: 0%;

    position: relative;
    /* Keeps the sheet's negative z-index inside this card rather than letting
       it slide behind the page ground. */
    isolation: isolate;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.875rem;
    height: 100%;
    transition: transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     THE SHEET.

     One pseudo-element carrying the entire physical event: a panel of raised
     paper, a hairline edge, an ink shadow, and a raking light across it. It
     bleeds outside the card box so the lift reads without any text moving, and
     the only property that transitions is `opacity`, so the state change costs
     one composited layer rather than a relayout.

     ── Why the gradient is shaded and not tinted ──────────────────────────────
     `--color-paper-raised` on the `--color-paper` ground is a fraction of a
     step of contrast; a pool of it is not a highlight, it is nothing, which is
     exactly what the previous version measured as. A gradient needs somewhere
     to travel, and the only direction available on a paper ground is *down*.
     So the lit end is `--color-paper-raised` and the shaded end is a 7% mix
     toward `--color-ink`: the sheet is shaded away from the light rather than
     brightened towards it, which is also what a real sheet under a real lamp
     does.

     7% is a measured ceiling, not a taste. Measured on a real build, that mix
     resolves to #deddd9, and every foreground this card puts on it holds:

       --color-ink   (headings, domain labels)   13.20:1  AAA
       --color-slate (summary, credits, stack)    4.94:1  AA
       --color-accent (the armed CTA)             5.60:1  AA
       domain mark, 68% mix (worst: fpga)         3.13:1  passes 1.4.11
       --color-rule  (hairlines)                  2.82:1  FAILS 1.4.11

     That last line is why `.card__media` and `.card__stack-item` darken their
     borders to `--color-slate` (4.94:1) whenever the sheet is showing, and why
     `.card__channel-rule` goes to `--color-ink`. The only `--color-rule` left
     is this pseudo-element's own border, and that one is a boundary *between*
     the sheet and the page rather than a mark *on* the sheet — it reads at
     3.07:1 against the paper it separates the sheet from, which is the pair
     1.4.11 is about.

     7% is therefore a ceiling in two directions at once: past it
     `--color-slate` drops under 4.5:1 and the fpga channel mark drops under
     3:1. Do not raise it without re-measuring every row above.

     The far stop is `--color-paper-raised` and NOT `transparent`. `transparent`
     is `rgba(0,0,0,0)`, and interpolating an opaque colour towards it in an
     unpremultiplied space drags every intermediate stop toward black — a grey
     halo around the highlight, on a surface whose entire subject is clean
     paper.

     ── Why there is a shadow at all ──────────────────────────────────────────
     "Lifted" with no shadow is a 3px jump. The audit of the deployed build
     found the hover changed no shadow, no border and no background, and it was
     right: the only thing a visitor could actually see move was the text. The
     two-layer shadow is the contact edge plus the cast — mixed from
     `--color-ink`, so no colour value is introduced here either.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .card::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: calc(-1 * var(--spacing-gutter)) calc(-0.75 * var(--spacing-gutter));
    z-index: -1;
    border: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--color-paper-raised);
    background-image: radial-gradient(
      88% 74% at var(--card-x) var(--card-y),
      var(--color-paper-raised) 0%,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-ink) 3%, var(--color-paper-raised)) 46%,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-ink) 7%, var(--color-paper-raised)) 100%
    );
    box-shadow:
      0 1px 1px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-ink) 10%, transparent),
      0 10px 22px -12px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-ink) 38%, transparent),
      0 24px 48px -28px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-ink) 30%, transparent);
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .card:hover,
  .card:focus-within {
    transform: translateY(-3px);
  }

  /* :focus-within is listed alongside :hover on every rule in this file. If a
     pointer can see it, a keyboard can see it — and because the pointer's only
     privilege is *where* the light sits, the two states are identical in
     everything but that. `.card__link` is the first focusable element inside
     the card and its stretched `::after` is the whole hit area, so both
     selectors are driven by the same anchor. */
  .card:hover::before,
  .card:focus-within::before {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Masthead: domain channels + figure number
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .card__channels {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 3px;
    width: 100%;
    padding-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .card__channel {
    width: 2.25rem;
    height: var(--border-rule);
    /* NON-TEXT ONLY. The oscilloscope accents are balanced for the ink ground,
       so on paper they are mixed toward ink to recover contrast (tokens.css
       carries the measured table and the boxed warning).

       68% measures 3.41-4.90:1 on paper: fpga 3.41 / ai 3.51 / quantum 3.88 /
       audio 4.34 / electronics 5.12. That clears the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 needs of a
       graphic mark, with headroom -- the 73% ceiling would put fpga at 3.04,
       which is too close to the floor for a 2px bar to be worth the saturation.

       It does NOT clear 4.5:1, so this ratio must never be moved onto a `color`
       property. Domain-coloured TEXT requires the 56% mix (4.51-6.14:1). No text
       in this component is domain-tinted; see .card__tags--domains. */
    background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--domain-accent) 68%, var(--color-ink));
  }

  .card__channel-rule {
    flex: 1;
    height: 0;
    border-top: var(--border-rule) solid var(--color-rule);
    transition: border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .card:hover .card__channel-rule,
  .card:focus-within .card__channel-rule {
    border-top-color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .card__figure {
    padding-left: 0.75rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-stretch: normal;
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    color: var(--color-slate);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Media — only rendered when a project actually has a cover.

     There is no placeholder branch. A project without a cover is a typographic
     entry, not a card with a hole in it: the masthead channels and an enlarged
     title carry the visual weight instead (see `.card--typographic`).
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .card__media {
    width: 100%;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--color-paper-raised);
    transition: border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
    view-transition-name: var(--vt-media, attr(data-vt type(<custom-ident>), none));
  }

  /* The figure's frame firms up with the rest of the card — and it has to.
     `--color-rule` measures 2.82:1 against the sheet's shaded end, under the
     3:1 that WCAG 1.4.11 asks of a boundary; `--color-slate` measures 4.94:1.
     See the contrast table above `.card::before`. */
  .card:hover .card__media,
  .card:focus-within .card__media {
    border-color: var(--color-slate);
  }

  /* `<Image />` renders its own <img>, so it is reached through the wrapper
     rather than through a class the scoper may or may not forward. `<Image />`
     already emits width/height attributes from the source's `ImageMetadata`;
     the reserved aspect box here means the card cannot shift even before the
     bytes arrive (audit: raw <img> without dimensions was the CLS finding).

     `contain`, not `cover`. Every cover on this site is a figure lifted from
     the repository that produced it — a forecast plot, a confusion matrix —
     and the axis labels, the class names and the legend *are* the evidence.
     `cover` crops them off; `fill` distorts the geometry a reader is being
     asked to trust. So the figure is fitted whole into the box and the
     remainder is paper. Two boxes exist, `standard` and `wide`, chosen in the
     frontmatter from the source's own ratio, which keeps a listing to two
     media heights while letting a 2.5:1 plot sit in something near its own
     shape. Both are fixed ratios, so CLS is zero either way. */
  .card__media :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
    object-fit: contain;
  }

  .card__media[data-shape='wide'] :global(img) {
    aspect-ratio: 5 / 2;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Title — the one real link, stretched over the whole card
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .card__title {
    font-size: var(--text-display-4);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     View-transition idents, read from an attribute by static CSS.

     The ident must be unique per document and identical across the two
     documents being paired, so it is per-slug and cannot be a fixed literal.
     Every other way of getting a per-element value into CSS is closed here:

       · an inline `style` attribute is DROPPED. `security.csp` emits
         `style-src` with hashes and no `style-src-attr`; `style-src-attr` falls
         back to `style-src`, and a hash only ever authorises a `<style>`
         element, never an attribute. Verified on a real build.
       · a generated `<style is:inline>` is dropped too — verified: Astro hashes
         only the style elements it compiles itself, so the element's sha256 is
         absent from the policy.
       · `Astro.csp.insertStyleHash()` cannot rescue that. `renderAllHeadContent`
         serialises the policy at the moment `<head>` renders, which is before
         any body component runs, so the call is a silent no-op from here.

     So the value comes from `data-vt` and the rule stays completely static, in
     the bundled stylesheet, under plain `style-src 'self'`.

     `var(--vt-title, …)` wraps it deliberately: `global.css` nulls `--vt-title`
     with `!important` under reduced motion, and keeping the property in the
     chain means that guard still wins with no change to the other agent's file.

     Advanced `attr()` is Chromium-only for now. Where it is unsupported the
     declaration is invalid at computed-value time, `view-transition-name`
     resolves to `none`, and the browser performs an ordinary navigation — the
     same graceful degradation as a browser with no view transitions at all.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  .card__title-text {
    view-transition-name: var(--vt-title, attr(data-vt type(<custom-ident>), none));
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     The link's own state.

     `.card__link` is the element that is actually focusable and actually
     clickable — its `::after` is the card's whole hit area — and until now it
     carried no `:hover` and no `:focus-visible` of its own at all. Everything
     visible on hover was hung off the `<article>`, which is not the control.
     A live inspection of the deployed build reported a `transition: all` here
     as the cause; that reading was wrong (`all` is the *initial* value of
     `transition-property`, so it is what `getComputedStyle` returns for an
     element with no transition whatsoever), but the missing state was real.

     A hairline rule in `--color-ink`, and not the two obvious alternatives.
     Ultramarine was tried first and rendered: a three-line, 40px heading turned
     entirely to the accent reads as an unvisited hyperlink rather than as an
     armed affordance, and on a card whose cover happens to be a blue plot it
     fights the evidence for attention. A colour change on the text itself was
     the same problem in a different form. The rule keeps the title in ink,
     where the whole system puts its headings, and says "link" in the register
     the rest of the page already uses — `.link`, `.selected__all` and
     `.card__external` are all underlined ink or slate.

     Reserved at rest with a transparent decoration colour rather than switched
     on with `text-decoration-line`, so arming the link changes only a paint and
     cannot reflow a heading that wraps.

     `--color-transparent` rather than the bare keyword: tokens.css is the only
     file in this repository allowed to name a colour, keyword or otherwise.

     Hovering anywhere on the card triggers this, because the stretched
     `::after` is what the pointer is over. That is the intended reading — the
     whole card is one link, and this says so. The two external links sit above
     that overlay with their own `z-index`, so hovering `GITHUB` correctly does
     *not* arm the case-study link. */

  .card__link {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--color-transparent);
    text-decoration-thickness: var(--border-hairline);
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
    transition: text-decoration-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .card__link:hover,
  .card__link:focus-visible {
    text-decoration-color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .card__link::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }

  /* The ring belongs around the card, not around the title's text box — but the
     focused element is the anchor. Moving the outline onto the stretched
     pseudo-element gives the correct visual without needing :has(). */
  .card__link:focus-visible {
    outline: none;
  }

  .card__link:focus-visible::after {
    outline: var(--border-rule) solid var(--focus-ring);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Copy and metadata
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .card__summary {
    max-width: var(--container-prose);
    color: var(--color-slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .card__credits {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-stretch: normal;
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    color: var(--color-slate);
  }

  .card__tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.375rem 0.875rem;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-stretch: normal;
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .card__tags--domains {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .card__domain {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* The one emphatic case tokens.css reserves --border-heavy for: a domain
     channel marker. An empty, aria-hidden 3px bar, purely redundant with the
     label beside it -- which is why it may sit at the non-text mix.

     NON-TEXT ONLY, 68% = 3.41-4.90:1 on paper. The `--domain-accent` custom
     property is *declared* on `.card__domain` (a text-bearing <li>) but is
     consumed only here, as a background. The label's own colour comes from
     `.card__tags--domains` and is --color-ink at 14.38:1. Tinting that text
     would require the 56% mix; do not reach for this variable to do it. */
  .card__domain-mark {
    width: var(--border-heavy);
    height: 0.75em;
    background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--domain-accent) 68%, var(--color-ink));
  }

  .card__tags--stack {
    color: var(--color-slate);
    gap: 0.375rem 0.75rem;
  }

  .card__stack-item {
    padding: 0.125rem 0.5rem;
    border: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    transition: border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .card:hover .card__stack-item,
  .card:focus-within .card__stack-item {
    border-color: var(--color-slate);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Foot: the sighted affordance, plus the genuinely separate destinations
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .card__foot {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    width: 100%;
    /* Pins the action row to the bottom so a row of cards lines up. */
    margin-top: auto;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .card__cta,
  .card__external {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5em;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-stretch: normal;
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .card__cta {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    transition: color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .card:hover .card__cta,
  .card:focus-within .card__cta {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .card__arrow {
    transition: transform var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .card:hover .card__arrow,
  .card:focus-within .card__arrow {
    transform: translateX(0.25em);
  }

  .card__links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1rem;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    /* Above the stretched overlay, so these stay clickable and keep their own
       tab stops after the card's own link. */
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
  }

  .card__external {
    color: var(--color-slate);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: from-font;
    text-underline-offset: 0.3em;
    transition: color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .card__external:hover,
  .card__external:focus-visible {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .card__external-mark {
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  /* No cover: the title is the image. Slightly more air and a heavier masthead
     so the entry reads as deliberate typography rather than a missing slot. */
  .card--typographic .card__channels {
    padding-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Touch: the card starts in the stronger state (brief §33).
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  @media (hover: none) {
    .card__channel-rule {
      border-top-color: var(--color-ink);
    }

    .card__stack-item,
    .card__media {
      border-color: var(--color-slate);
    }

    /* The sheet is simply *there*, permanently — the listing becomes a stack of
       physical sheets rather than four regions of the same page, and every
       affordance a pointer user gets by hovering is already on screen
       (MOTION_SYSTEM §7: "the card itself is the affordance").

       The cast shadow is dropped and only the contact edge kept, because a
       lifted sheet would be claiming a state that no longer exists here: there
       is nothing to hover, so nothing has risen. Flat on the page, with a
       boundary. */
    .card::before {
      opacity: 1;
      box-shadow: 0 1px 1px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-ink) 10%, transparent);
    }

    .card:hover,
    .card:focus-within {
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Reduced motion.

     Durations already collapse globally (global.css), so the state change is
     instant rather than removed — nothing disappears and nothing waits on an
     animation.

     What is withdrawn is the *movement*: the lift, and the light's travel. The
     pointer script in `ProjectGrid.astro` never attaches under this query, so
     `--card-x` / `--card-y` keep the resting values declared at the top of this
     file and the light simply stays where it is.

     Everything the hover actually communicates survives untouched — the raised
     panel, the hairline edge, the ink shadow, the masthead rule darkening, the
     accent CTA, the armed link underline. That is deliberate and it is the
     point: a card that speaks only through motion says nothing at all to the
     visitor who asked for less of it.

     The cross-document view transition is switched off too — an unnamed
     element is simply not paired, and the browser performs an ordinary
     navigation.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .card__title-text,
    .card__media {
      view-transition-name: none;
    }

    .card:hover,
    .card:focus-within {
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  /* --- work-in-progress badge ----------------------------------------------
     Deliberately the same object as `.detail__status`: a reader meeting the
     card and then the page should not have to work out whether two differently
     drawn labels mean the same thing. Accent dot, hairline border, mono micro
     type — the site's existing vocabulary for a piece of live status.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .card__status {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    align-self: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 0.125rem;
    padding: 0.125rem 0.5rem;
    border: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-stretch: normal;
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .card__status-dot {
    width: 0.5rem;
    height: 0.5rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    background-color: var(--color-accent);
  }
</style>
